# XCU 1.5 Considerations for Utilities in Support of Files of Arbitrary Size
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# A sparse file just over 4 GiB is created with dd; where the file system or
# limits do not allow it the tests report that the resource is not in use.

assertion  XCU.1.5-large-files.001
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.5/The following utilities support files of any size up to the maximum that can be created by the implementation.
class      required
since      2008
says       For a file larger than 4 GiB (a sparse file of 4294967297 bytes), ls
           -l prints the exact size, test -s succeeds, find -size matches the
           exact block and byte counts, and du and df succeed.
timeout    120
test       t/001-size-reporting.sh

assertion  XCU.1.5-large-files.002
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.5/The following utilities support files of any size up to the maximum that can be created by the implementation.
class      required
since      2008
says       dd interprets skip, seek and count values that place data beyond 4
           GiB: reading with skip at 4294967296 bytes returns the byte stored
           there, and writing with seek past 4 GiB gives a file of the expected
           size.
timeout    120
test       t/002-dd-offsets.sh

assertion  XCU.1.5-large-files.003
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.5/The following utilities support files of any size up to the maximum that can be created by the implementation.
class      required
since      2008
says       cmp -l reports byte numbers above 4 GiB: two files of 4294967297 bytes
           that differ only in the last byte give byte number 4294967297 with the
           two values.
timeout    300
test       t/003-cmp-offsets.sh

assertion  XCU.1.5-large-files.004
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.5/The following utilities support files of any size up to the maximum that can be created by the implementation.
class      required
since      2008
says       cksum reports the exact size of a file larger than 4 GiB and the same
           checksum whether it reads the file or its content through a pipe from
           cat.
timeout    300
test       t/004-cksum-size.sh

assertion  XCU.1.5-large-files.005
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.5/Set or report file size limit.
class      required
since      2008
says       ulimit -f accepts a value above 2^32 and reports the same value back,
           and with a limit just above the size of a file of 4294967297 bytes, a
           write that produces such a file succeeds.
timeout    60
test       t/005-ulimit-large.sh

assertion  XCU.1.5-large-files.006
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.5/The following utilities support files of any size up to the maximum that can be created by the implementation.
class      required
since      2008
says       touch, chmod, chown, chgrp, ln, mv and rm operate on a file larger
           than 4 GiB without changing its size: the size is still 4294967297
           bytes after each, the link count follows ln and rm.
timeout    120
test       t/006-file-handling.sh
